ed to talk about  audience all right  this may seem to be a bit of a no dub  moment but in the film experience there  is a film on the screen and the audience  watching it think of the screen as a  person that is telling the film to the  audience the majority of film uses  narrative in order to transmit the film  to the audience in a way that the  audience can follow and interpret while  in a fixed timeframe some of the NuWave  outers took it as an opportunity to  learn how far they could stretch and  pull the medium while still keeping the  audience engaged while others took it to  see how far they could stretch and pull  the audience while still keeping the  film intact others disregarded the  audience and medium altogether and made  it more about the discussion about the  film politics agenda and gender that  exist around the film though not  necessarily within the film itself how  to explain this in a more manageable way  okay a film that stretches the medium  may play with things like in congruous  settings timeline jumps fourth wall  breaking moments or visual illusions  that exploit the production aspect of  the film one that stretches the audience  may not do anything extreme in terms of  editing or construction but may use  demanding metaphors sparse dialogue and  ambiguous figure language pushing the  audience to keep up or interpret what's  going on many such as 2001 do both  whether you love hate or know nothing  about French new-wave  if you're a film fan then you've  benefited from the children of New Wave  from the first camp of out tours the  medium focused ones filmmakers learned  that you could actually stretch the  medium pretty far and still keep the  audience's willing participants and from  that we get the kind of styles used by  Tarantino Aronofsky and Fincher on the  other foot you get the Cohens Jarmusch  and Wes Anderson and they're difficult  to like characters films about nothing  and stories that just happen there that  brings us up to speed so we can actually  talk about Enter the Void Enter the Void  is a 2009 French film written and  directed by Gaspar know about an  American drug dealer living in Tokyo the  main character is Oscar a hollow waste  of space with no goals or ambition who  was shot in the drug raid when his gay  friend betrays him to the police after  finding out that Oscar had sex with his  mother the bulk of its 155 minutes is  spent following Oscars consciousness  from a detached first-person perspective  as it experiences stuff happening like I  said before there is no plot the  characters have no goals nothing has  worked towards a wrist and there's no  narrative climax in terms of how far it  stretches the audience in the medium  it's not the furthest thing out there  but it's still pretty close to the edge  of what even most arthouse film fans can  tolerate this is difficult to explain  because it's a cumulative effect that  builds up while watching the movie at  any given moment it feels like something  is happening but nothing ever goes  anywhere events are disjointed the  timeline is muddled the visuals are  alienating the music is unsettling the  events are boring depressing shocking  and disgusting but nothing sticks  eventually you lose track of where you  are in the movie 45 minutes have passed  and you don't remember what happened in  them this is intentional on the part of  the director and part of the overall  purpose the film has gained some  notoriety for its depiction of drug use  specifically the first-person depiction  of a dose of DMT or dimethyltryptamine  DMT is a powerful psychedelic  hallucinogen similar to LSD or  psilocybin renowned for the complex  visual fields and fractal imagery  generated during  experience according to many who have  taken DMT the visuals here are among the  better interpretations of the experience  DMT is a major active ingredient in  ayahuasca and Anthea genic drink used by  Amazonian tribes to commune with God's  primal spirits in the inner self and to  practice dying  throughout history psychedelics have  been used to search for higher planes of  existence and comprehend one's place  within the universe a use that is not  unfounded as even in the 20th century  medical researchers discovered the  patients with terminal diseases found  themselves better emotionally equipped  to handle their imminent death after  being administered a moderate dose of  psilocybin the active chemical in magic  mushrooms this concept of practicing  dying is also found in the meditative  practices of yoga including within the  Tibetan sect of Buddhism Bardo the doll  aka the Tibetan Book of the Dead is a  collection of funerary texts that  describes the souls journey after death  as a experiences first the overwhelming  fulness of reality which the impure soul  rejects as incomprehensible and a pure  soul accepts thus accepting Nirvana the  impure soul having been incapable of  ascension wanders the earth and  encounters the forms of the Buddha or  others who have lived above the soul in  wholeness where the soul is shown by  example what it must do to progress  after this if the soul lingers without a  body it begins to experience a form of  entropy karmically impelled  hallucinations which drive the soul to  seek a new body and thus rebirth this  process is sometimes described as a  great spiraling dance through eternity  and while it is not without pain and  suffering it is ultimately a celebration  of life and an idealization of harmony  and perfection it is not unique either  the image of spirals fiery wheels and  cosmic dances are recurrent in virtually  all religions worldwide superficially it  appears that this is what is at the core  of enter the void the thread that  ultimately gives structure to the  narrative that Oscars spirit leaves his  body and experiences the failure to  achieve enlightenment tutelage karma and  rebirth this is where our discussion of  enter the void starts to get really  rough because enter the void isn't about  Tibetan spiritual journeys the  durability and duty of the soul or  harmony  gasbar no has denied that it's about  Oscar soul seeking rebirth and insisted  that it's a film about a man who just  heard about the  Metin Book of the Dead before being shot  and is hallucinating one last time  before slipping into oblivion  in many ways it is a continuation of the  theme that no explored in his previous  film 2002's  irreversible which ended with the title  card time destroys everything knows  films have very much wallowed in entropy  focusing on dark violent characters  whose decisions lead only to misery and  destruction leaving them ultimately  alone and vulnerable the film is ugly  the characters damned and self damned  twice over and the hole is tinged with a  ponderous defeatism it is difficult and  exhausting to watch because what should  be the thread that guides us through is  treated with derision by the film itself  the subject matter is bleak and it  builds only to nothingness it is a  painful film in both construction and  content but isn't that the point like I  said the discussion gets rough here  because the film invites us to step  outside of it and ask questions about  the nature of the film as an experience  for example does a film need to be a  narrative and what does it say about us  the audience when we attempt to impose a  narrative where one has been  intentionally denied should the  statement and intent of the outer take  ultimate precedence over the  interpretation of the audience what does  our willingness to accept or deny the  outers intent say about our opinions on  the subject matter on a more grounded  front enter the void challenges us to  figure out for ourselves what makes a  film good or bad and question what a  film even needs to be does it need a  plot or can a film be something you just  sit and experience fantasia  Koyaanisqatsi and 7 million pictures  think so if the film sets out to confuse  offend and alienate does that make it  good if it succeeds negative emotions  are as valid and real as positive ones  and a good cry is often what it takes to  bring the world into a bit of  perspective in the end whether Gaspard  knows says it's about nothingness or  rebirth Enter the Void is about death it  is in a sense a modern atheistic version  of the Book of the Dead a mediation on  the nature of death a look at our  cultural understanding of what it means  to die and viewing the film as a form of  practicing dying the ultimate metaphor  that encapsulates the film is a bad drug  trip the view  has lost control of the experience and  has no choice but to accept the reality  their subconscious has served up for  better or for worse and learn what it's  like to die cutting through the  sensationalist imagery and the long  unfocused running time I believe that  this is the most challenging aspect of  the film and it's redeeming value as a  piece of art mortality is a universal  human experience and yet we so often  treat it as a villain to be outwitted or  a disease to be cured  gasbar know has set out to drag us  kicking and screaming face to face with  our own demise you may not be  comfortable with his fatalist conclusion  that we all must at one point or another  enter the void but that is the  opportunity that art affords us the  chance of better understanding ourselves  by forcing us to confront the myriad  aspects of the human experience and make  decisions to get us thinking and  disagreeing and raising our hand to say  no I don't think life needs to be  hopeless now if only there were another  film that asks these kinds of deep  important questions about mortality and  wasn't like being slowly crushed by  industrial machinery special trace  approach  they say whoever drinks of itself within  forever  oh thank goodness  you
 

